15:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1d",null,{"guide":{"date":"June 27, 1862","place":"Gaines’s Mill battlefield north of the Chickahominy River, near Mechanicsville and Richmond in Hanover County, Virginia","result":"Confederate victory. A late assault by Robert E. Lee’s army broke Fitz John Porter’s reinforced V Corps line behind Boatswain’s Swamp. Porter’s survivors withdrew across the Chickahominy after dark. The victory cost Lee about 8,750 casualties and came too late in the day to destroy Porter’s command","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"The final assault, Federal retreat, and command assessment at Gaines’s Mill","note":"This episode resumes the June 27 battle after earlier Confederate attacks had failed to break Porter’s position."},{"label":"Battlefield position","value":"Porter faced west and north behind Boatswain’s Swamp with the Chickahominy at his rear","note":"The National Park Service describes a defensive arc about one and one-half miles long. The episode describes the late line as part of a front extending roughly two miles."},{"label":"Late Confederate front","value":"D. H. Hill on the left; Ewell, Winder, Lawton, and A. P. Hill in the center; Whiting and Longstreet on the right","note":"The alignment was assembled after delayed marches, earlier attacks, and piecemeal deployment. Exact brigade boundaries shifted during the fighting."},{"label":"Whiting enters the line","value":"Lee placed Whiting to the right of A. P. Hill after 5 p.m.","note":"Whiting’s division, with John B. Hood’s brigade on the left and Evander M. Law’s on the right, filled much of the gap between A. P. Hill and Longstreet."},{"label":"Jackson’s role","value":"Jackson ordered his divisions forward but did not personally place them for the final assault","note":"Lee had already directed Ewell and Whiting into the battle. The episode treats Jackson’s delayed march and limited battlefield direction as a serious failure."},{"label":"Final attack strength in the episode","value":"About 32,000 Confederates against roughly 32,000–34,000 United States troops","note":"These figures describe forces available along the late-day front, not total forces present during the whole battle. The National Park Service lists 34,214 United States and 57,018 Confederate personnel engaged by its broader accounting."},{"label":"Federal command problem","value":"Porter had almost no organized reserve when the final assault developed","note":"Regiments from McCall’s and Slocum’s divisions had been inserted where needed, reducing the ability of their division and brigade commanders to control intact formations during a breakthrough."},{"label":"Porter’s right","value":"George Sykes’s regulars, reinforced by Joseph J. Bartlett’s brigade","note":"Sykes’s brigades were commanded by Robert C. Buchanan, Charles S. Lovell, and Gouverneur K. Warren. They faced D. H. Hill and elements of Jackson’s command."},{"label":"Porter’s left","value":"George W. Morell’s division on the Watt House plateau","note":"The brigades of Charles Griffin, John H. Martindale, and Daniel Butterfield, with attached units from McCall and Slocum, faced Whiting and Longstreet."},{"label":"D. H. Hill’s advance","value":"A slow attack through wooded, boggy ground fixed Sykes’s division","note":"Hill made limited progress for more than an hour, but the pressure helped prevent Sykes from reinforcing other sectors."},{"label":"20th North Carolina losses","value":"272 casualties in the episode’s account","note":"The regiment, part of Samuel Garland’s brigade, was in its first battle. The episode gives the loss as about 40 percent of its strength."},{"label":"Lawton’s brigade","value":"About 3,600 men in its first combat","note":"Lawton’s large Georgia brigade crossed the swamp after a prolonged struggle, gained the Federal side, and paused to restore alignment in smoke and fading light."},{"label":"Longstreet’s attack","value":"Five brigades advanced from the Powhite Creek area toward the Federal left","note":"Longstreet first began a diversion, then changed to a full assault after Lee required stronger action."},{"label":"Whiting’s attack order","value":"Law and Hood were to advance rapidly without stopping to fire","note":"The National Park Service confirms that speed and momentum were central to Hood’s instructions. Hood led the 4th Texas and 18th Georgia toward the creek."},{"label":"George E. Pickett wounded","value":"Shot near the right shoulder during Longstreet’s advance","note":"The National Park Service trail guide states that Pickett was hit after his brigade halted under heavy fire near the creek. He survived."},{"label":"Breakthrough","value":"The Federal line failed in several sectors at nearly the same time","note":"Hood’s, Wilcox’s, Lawton’s, and D. H. Hill’s troops later advanced competing claims. The evidence does not establish a precise first breakthrough across the entire front."},{"label":"Federal captures","value":"Nearly 1,000 men from two regiments of George W. Taylor’s brigade were captured","note":"Other pockets of United States troops were also cut off during the retreat, especially near the breakthrough on the Federal left."},{"label":"Forward artillery losses","value":"Ten United States guns were abandoned along the main line in the episode’s account","note":"Confederate infantry closed on the batteries as supporting Federal infantry withdrew and battery horses were shot."},{"label":"Cooke’s cavalry charge","value":"About 250 United States cavalrymen attacked shortly after 8 p.m.","note":"Philip St. George Cooke ordered the charge without Porter’s authorization. Its repulse disrupted the reserve artillery position and cost about 50 troopers in the episode’s account."},{"label":"Reserve artillery losses","value":"Nine additional cannon were abandoned after the cavalry charge","note":"The episode treats the charge as a costly local action, not the cause of Porter’s already developing retreat."},{"label":"Late reinforcements","value":"Two brigades from Edwin V. Sumner’s II Corps arrived near 8 p.m.","note":"They arrived too late to restore the main position and instead helped cover the withdrawal toward the river."},{"label":"Night withdrawal","value":"Porter’s command crossed the Alexander and Grapevine bridges","note":"Darkness prevented Lee from fully exploiting the breakthrough and allowed the remnants of V Corps to rejoin the main army south of the Chickahominy."},{"label":"Battle casualties","value":"United States 6,837; Confederacy 8,750","note":"These are the National Park Service battle-summary figures. The episode rounds Porter’s loss to nearly 7,000 and Richmond National Battlefield Park rounds the Confederate loss to approximately 9,000. Counts vary by source and method."},{"label":"Operational consequence","value":"McClellan abandoned the Richmond and York River Railroad supply line and continued toward the James River","note":"The National Park Service identifies Gaines’s Mill as the defeat that confirmed McClellan’s decision to change his base and retreat away from Richmond."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"“The Richmond Campaign of 1862: The Peninsula and the Seven Days,” edited by Gary W. Gallagher","note":"The episode recommends this essay collection for further study of the campaign."},{"label":"Transcript normalizations","value":"Boatswain’s Swamp, Ewell, Winder, Morell, McGehee House, McDowell, and Fitz John Porter","note":"The automated transcript produces forms including “Bose and Swamp,” “Yule,” “Whinder,” “Morrill,” “McGaughey,” “Urban McDowell,” and “Vitch John Porter.”"}],"maps":[{"title":"Gaines’s Mill in the Peninsula Campaign","caption":"Gaines’s Mill lay north of the Chickahominy, about seven miles northeast of central Richmond. Porter’s isolated wing occupied the battlefield while most of McClellan’s army remained south of the river. Rivers, coastline, routes, locations, and distances are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep161-virginia-campaign-context.svg","alt":"Schematic regional map of Virginia showing Washington, Richmond, the Virginia Peninsula, Fort Monroe, the Chickahominy River, and Gaines’s Mill."},{"title":"The final assault at Gaines’s Mill","caption":"D. H. Hill pressed the Federal right while Winder, Lawton, Ewell, and remnants of A. P. Hill attacked the center. Whiting and Longstreet advanced against Morell’s left near the Watt House. The Federal line broke in several places before withdrawing toward the Chickahominy bridges. Unit blocks and arrows show broad relationships, not exact formations, timing, frontage, or regimental routes. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Robert Knox Sneden map, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep161-gaines-mill-final-assault.svg","alt":"Schematic map of the final Confederate assault across Boatswain’s Swamp and the Federal withdrawal toward the Chickahominy River."},{"title":"Robert Knox Sneden’s period map of Gaines’s Mill","caption":"Sneden’s wartime map shows the Chickahominy, Powhite Creek, Boatswain’s Swamp, the Watt House plateau, roads, and colored force positions. Its title gives June 26, but the Library of Congress notes that Gaines’s Mill was fought on June 27. The map is a historical artifact rather than a modern surveyed reconstruction.","source":"Library of Congress Geography and Map Division; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep161-sneden-gaines-mill-map.jpg","alt":"Robert Knox Sneden watercolor map of the Battle of Gaines’s Mill with Union and Confederate positions marked in color."}],"moments":[{"time":"0:30","audioTime":30,"title":"The episode returns to Gaines’s Mill","detail":"The action resumes late on Friday, June 27, with Lee trying to concentrate his army before dark."},{"time":"1:44","audioTime":104,"title":"Jackson calls for his remaining divisions","detail":"A confused message delays Whiting until Robert L. Dabney clarifies the order."},{"time":"2:58","audioTime":178,"title":"Lee places Whiting after 5 p.m.","detail":"Whiting moves between A. P. Hill and Longstreet with Hood on the left and Law on the right."},{"time":"3:25","audioTime":205,"title":"Whiting and Longstreet coordinate","detail":"Their late attacks produce the strongest concentration yet against the Federal line."},{"time":"4:12","audioTime":252,"title":"Lee meets Jackson","detail":"Lee addresses Jackson’s late arrival and discusses the need for a strong attack."},{"time":"5:04","audioTime":304,"title":"Jackson orders renewed pressure","detail":"He sends orders to advance, although Lee has already committed Ewell and Whiting."},{"time":"5:50","audioTime":350,"title":"The Confederate front is identified","detail":"Longstreet and Whiting hold the right, A. P. Hill and Ewell the center, and D. H. Hill the far left."},{"time":"6:18","audioTime":378,"title":"Winder and Lawton enter the center","detail":"Their brigades move near Ewell while other Winder units remain in reserve farther south."},{"time":"6:42","audioTime":402,"title":"Pressure develops on both flanks","detail":"D. H. Hill is already advancing, and Longstreet converts his diversion into a full assault."},{"time":"7:05","audioTime":425,"title":"Lee has a nearly continuous front","detail":"The episode estimates about 32,000 Confederates available across more than two miles."},{"time":"8:07","audioTime":487,"title":"Porter has no effective reserve","detail":"His exhausted command contains attached regiments distributed piecemeal along the line."},{"time":"9:47","audioTime":587,"title":"The Federal right is described","detail":"Sykes’s regular brigades and Bartlett’s reinforcement face the Confederate left and center."},{"time":"10:18","audioTime":618,"title":"The Federal left is described","detail":"Morell’s brigades occupy the Watt House plateau opposite Whiting and Longstreet."},{"time":"11:32","audioTime":692,"title":"The final attack has no single start","detail":"Separate Confederate advances develop into a broad assault rather than beginning at one signal."},{"time":"12:02","audioTime":722,"title":"D. H. Hill attacks through difficult ground","detail":"His brigades make limited progress but keep much of Sykes’s division engaged."},{"time":"12:40","audioTime":760,"title":"Longstreet expands the diversion","detail":"Five brigades prepare for a full attack after Lee requires action beyond the original demonstration."},{"time":"13:25","audioTime":805,"title":"Winder and Lawton move forward","detail":"They attack near the ground where Ewell’s men had been repulsed earlier."},{"time":"13:57","audioTime":837,"title":"Sykes falls back toward the McGehee House","detail":"Confederate pressure and Federal counterattacks shift the line around the house."},{"time":"14:10","audioTime":850,"title":"The 20th North Carolina takes heavy losses","detail":"The episode reports 272 casualties, about 40 percent of the regiment."},{"time":"14:32","audioTime":872,"title":"Lawton’s brigade enters its first battle","detail":"About 3,600 Georgians press across the swamp and reach the plateau."},{"time":"16:28","audioTime":988,"title":"Longstreet and Whiting begin the coordinated assault","detail":"Their brigades cross exposed ground toward Boatswain’s Swamp under heavy fire."},{"time":"17:29","audioTime":1049,"title":"Lee speaks with Hood","detail":"Lee explains the position before Hood leads his brigade into the attack."},{"time":"18:33","audioTime":1113,"title":"Whiting orders a rapid advance","detail":"Law and Hood are directed to close without stopping to fire."},{"time":"19:09","audioTime":1149,"title":"Hood closes a gap","detail":"He leads the 4th Texas and 18th Georgia to connect Law’s brigade with Longstreet’s line."},{"time":"19:50","audioTime":1190,"title":"Hood orders the charge","detail":"The two regiments rush the creek as Longstreet’s troops advance in the adjacent sector."},{"time":"20:16","audioTime":1216,"title":"The Federal position breaks","detail":"Martindale’s troops give way, and Wilcox breaks into Butterfield’s sector at nearly the same time."},{"time":"21:06","audioTime":1266,"title":"Claims for the first breakthrough are assessed","detail":"The episode concludes that the line failed almost simultaneously across several sectors."},{"time":"22:15","audioTime":1335,"title":"The Federal retreat spreads","detail":"Some units withdraw in order while others flee or are cut off on the plateau."},{"time":"23:00","audioTime":1380,"title":"Confederates reach the artillery","detail":"Federal guns use canister at short range, but ten pieces are abandoned along the front."},{"time":"24:00","audioTime":1440,"title":"Cooke orders a cavalry charge","detail":"About 250 Federal cavalrymen attack after 8 p.m. and are driven back through the reserve batteries."},{"time":"25:02","audioTime":1502,"title":"Nine more guns are abandoned","detail":"The repulsed cavalry adds to the confusion around Porter’s reserve artillery."},{"time":"26:12","audioTime":1572,"title":"Late reinforcements cover the retreat","detail":"Two II Corps brigades arrive near 8 p.m. and help protect the withdrawal rather than restore the line."},{"time":"26:26","audioTime":1586,"title":"Porter moves toward the bridges","detail":"The defeated troops crowd the roads to the Alexander and Grapevine bridges as darkness ends pursuit."},{"time":"27:03","audioTime":1623,"title":"Hood receives credit for the breakthrough","detail":"The episode notes the 4th Texas’s prominent place in later accounts while recognizing competing claims."},{"time":"27:25","audioTime":1645,"title":"Confederate losses are summarized","detail":"The episode distributes severe losses across Whiting, A. P. Hill, Longstreet, D. H. Hill, Ewell, Winder, and Lawton."},{"time":"28:46","audioTime":1726,"title":"Jackson’s performance is assessed","detail":"His delayed march and limited direction of his divisions are identified as major command failures."},{"time":"30:34","audioTime":1834,"title":"Lee’s battlefield control is assessed","detail":"Despite earlier misjudgments, Lee assembles enough force to break the position before dark."},{"time":"30:58","audioTime":1858,"title":"McClellan’s conduct is assessed","detail":"McClellan remains south of the river, sends limited reinforcements, and leaves Porter to hold the exposed wing."},{"time":"32:36","audioTime":1956,"title":"The book recommendation begins","detail":"The recommended volume is Gary W. Gallagher’s edited collection on the Richmond Campaign of 1862."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Remained south of the Chickahominy, controlled reinforcement of Porter, and directed the broader change of base"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Fitz John Porter","command":"Reinforced V Corps","subordinates":"Held the line with divisions and attached units under Sykes, Morell, McCall, and Slocum"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. George Sykes","command":"V Corps division on the Federal right","subordinates":"Brigades of Robert C. Buchanan, Charles S. Lovell, and Gouverneur K. Warren"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. George W. Morell","command":"V Corps division on the Federal center and left","subordinates":"Brigades of Charles Griffin, John H. Martindale, and Daniel Butterfield"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. George A. McCall and Brig. Gen. Henry W. Slocum","command":"Attached divisions reinforcing Porter","subordinates":"Regiments were distributed along the line; Joseph J. Bartlett and George W. Taylor commanded prominent reinforcing brigades"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Philip St. George Cooke","command":"Cavalry attached to Porter","subordinates":"Ordered the late charge of about 250 troopers near the reserve artillery"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Directed the army’s concentration and late assault against Porter’s line"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","command":"Left wing of Lee’s army","subordinates":"Included divisions under Ewell, Whiting, Winder, and D. H. Hill; Lee often directed those formations without Jackson’s intervention"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. D. H. Hill","command":"Division on the Confederate far left","subordinates":"Five brigades attacked the Federal right through wooded and swampy ground"},{"commander":"Richard S. Ewell, Charles S. Winder, and Alexander R. Lawton","command":"Divisions and brigades in the Confederate center","subordinates":"Pressed Sykes’s line and joined the broad late-day advance"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. A. P. Hill","command":"Light Division","subordinates":"Surviving elements held the center after severe losses in earlier attacks"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. William H. C. Whiting","command":"Division between A. P. Hill and Longstreet","subordinates":"Brigades of John B. Hood and Evander M. Law"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. James Longstreet","command":"Division on the Confederate right","subordinates":"Committed five brigades, including those of Cadmus M. Wilcox, George E. Pickett, Roger A. Pryor, and Winfield S. Featherston"}]}],"people":[{"name":"John B. Hood","role":"Confederate brigade commander who led the 4th Texas and 18th Georgia across Boatswain’s Swamp","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/ep161-hood.jpg"},{"name":"Fitz John Porter","role":"United States V Corps commander responsible for the defense and night withdrawal","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep161-fitz-john-porter.jpg"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Confederate army commander who assembled and directed the late assault","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"George B. McClellan","role":"Army of the Potomac commander south of the Chickahominy","side":"United States"},{"name":"Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","role":"Confederate wing commander whose late movement and limited direction affected the battle","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"William H. C. Whiting","role":"Confederate division commander who ordered Law and Hood to close rapidly without firing","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"James Longstreet","role":"Confederate division commander whose five brigades attacked the Federal left","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"D. H. Hill","role":"Confederate division commander whose attack fixed and pushed back Sykes’s regulars","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"George Sykes","role":"United States division commander on Porter’s right","side":"United States"},{"name":"George W. Morell","role":"United States division commander on the Watt House plateau","side":"United States"},{"name":"Alexander R. Lawton","role":"Confederate commander of a large Georgia brigade in its first combat","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Evander M. Law","role":"Confederate brigade commander on Whiting’s right","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Cadmus M. Wilcox","role":"Confederate brigade commander whose Alabamians broke into Butterfield’s sector","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"George E. Pickett","role":"Confederate brigade commander wounded during Longstreet’s advance","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Philip St. George Cooke","role":"United States cavalry commander who ordered the late charge near the reserve artillery","side":"United States"}],"losses":[{"name":"Col. John Marshall","side":"Confederacy","role":"Commander, 4th Texas Infantry","outcome":"Killed by a long-range shot during the approach to Boatswain’s Swamp. The National Park Service trail guide places the event about 550 yards north of the creek."},{"name":"Col. Jesse A. Gove","side":"United States","role":"Commander, 22nd Massachusetts Infantry","outcome":"Killed while his regiment fought in Martindale’s brigade. His body was not recovered by the regiment after the Federal retreat."}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep161-virginia-campaign-context.svg","alt":"Schematic regional map locating Gaines’s Mill northeast of Richmond and north of the Chickahominy.","caption":"The battle occurred close to Richmond but on the north side of the Chickahominy, separated from most of McClellan’s army.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep161-gaines-mill-final-assault.svg","alt":"Schematic tactical map of Confederate attacks and the Federal retreat at Gaines’s Mill.","caption":"The broad assault placed pressure on Sykes’s right and Morell’s left at the same time. The diagram is schematic and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Robert Knox Sneden map, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep161-sneden-gaines-mill-map.jpg","alt":"Robert Knox Sneden watercolor map showing the Gaines’s Mill battlefield and colored unit positions.","caption":"Sneden’s period map preserves a participant-era interpretation. The Library of Congress corrects the June 26 date written in its title to June 27.","source":"Library of Congress Geography and Map Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep161-after-gaines-mill-waud.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud drawing of United States troops, wagons, artillery, and burning stores after Gaines’s Mill.","caption":"Alfred R. Waud drew Franklin’s VI Corps falling back near Trent’s House on June 29, with wagon traffic and burning commissary stores. It records the wider withdrawal after Gaines’s Mill, not the June 27 assault itself.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep161-hood.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era photographic portrait of Confederate officer John Bell Hood in uniform.","caption":"John B. Hood led the 4th Texas and 18th Georgia in the best-known breakthrough attack at Gaines’s Mill.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep161-fitz-john-porter.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era glass-negative portrait of United States general Fitz John Porter.","caption":"Fitz John Porter directed the reinforced V Corps defense and withdrew the survivors across the Chickahominy after dark.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 161 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.libsyn.com/161-seven-days-battles-gainess-mill-part-the-third"},{"label":"National Park Service battle summary: Gaines’s Mill","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va017"},{"label":"Richmond National Battlefield Park: The Battle of Gaines’s Mill","url":"https://www.nps.gov/rich/learn/historyculture/gainesmillbull.htm"},{"label":"Richmond National Battlefield Park: Gaines’s Mill Confederate Attack Trail","url":"https://www.nps.gov/rich/planyourvisit/upload/RICH-Gaines-Mill-2-21-2020finalpress-quality.pdf"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Virginia Campaigns, March–August 1862","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-5.pdf"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Robert Knox Sneden’s Plan of the Battle of Gaines’ Mill","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00217/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Alfred R. Waud’s “After Gaines Mill Sunday June 29th 1862”","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660316/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: John Bell Hood portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2022630550/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Fitz John Porter portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018667594/"},{"label":"John L. Parker, “Henry Wilson’s Regiment: History of the Twenty-Second Massachusetts Infantry”","url":"https://archive.org/details/henrywilsonsregi00park"},{"label":"Official podcast book-recommendations list","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"}]}}]}]
